The autocorrelation issue among adjacent sites was addressed by excluding sites with scene centroids within a 15 km radius, resulting in 8 spatially distinct site clusters for cross-validation. This was done to reduce autocorrelation. Testing showed that a 10 km radius resulted in the same number of clusters, whereas a 5 km radius was considered less reliable.
The model was trained multiple times using leave-one-site-out cross-validation (Fig. 2). This approach removes one site in each model iteration, serving as a held-out site for testing once the model is trained. It uses another site for internal validation to prevent data leakage. Early stopping is initialized once the best validation loss is satisfied. The model then restarts training on another iteration, but this time folds another site-cluster until all 8 clusters have been folded once, since the held-out cluster is touched only once [20], [46], [47]. Eight sites were used for leave-one-site-out cross-validation (1, 2, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12), while the remaining four sites (3, 4, 7, and 11) were not included in model training. The mean prediction metrics across the 8 site-cluster folds were derived with mean ± SD.
Geometric (random flips and rotations) and radiometric (small brightness/intensity changes in imagery) augmentations are applied. Every labeled scene is therefore predicted by exactly one model that has never seen this specific site (the fold model).
